---
import { getCategories } from '../../lib/catalog';

/**
 * The scrolling category band.
 *
 * WCAG 2.2.2 (Pause, Stop, Hide) applies: this starts automatically, moves, and
 * runs well past five seconds. That is a Level A failure without a pause
 * mechanism, and axe does not test for it — the same class of blind spot that
 * left a serious Label in Name failure on every product card at a green score.
 *
 * The control is a checkbox read by `:has()`, deliberately not an island: a
 * pause button that needs JavaScript to exist is not a mechanism for a visitor
 * who has JavaScript off, and the animation runs for them regardless.
 *
 * The track is duplicated and translated -50%, which is what makes the loop
 * seamless. Both copies are aria-hidden — the band is decorative, and every
 * category it names is a real link in the grid below.
 */
const categories = await getCategories();
const text = categories.map((c) => c.name).join(' · ') + ' · ';

/*
 * Each span carries two copies, not one.
 *
 * The loop is seamless only while one span is at least as wide as the viewport
 * — the track translates by exactly one span. A single copy of the fifteen
 * category names measures ~3218px in Archivo at 13px/800/0.3em, so the seam
 * opened on an ultrawide display, and it would open sooner if categories were
 * ever removed or renamed shorter. Doubling covers past 6400px, and the
 * duration doubles with it so the scroll speed is unchanged.
 */
const copy = text.repeat(2);
---

<div class="ticker">
  <div class="ticker__track" aria-hidden="true">
    <span>{copy}</span>
    <span>{copy}</span>
  </div>

  <!-- The track above is aria-hidden, so a control named bare "Pause" would
       have no referent anywhere on the page for a screen reader user — the
       aria-label restates what is being paused. It still has to start with
       the visible label text ("Pause"), or the mismatch is itself a 2.5.3
       Label in Name failure. -->
  <input
    class="ticker__toggle"
    type="checkbox"
    id="ticker-pause"
    role="switch"
    aria-label="Pause the scrolling category band"
  />
  <label class="ticker__btn" for="ticker-pause">Pause</label>
</div>

<style>
  .ticker {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--color-red-fill);
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: 13px 0;
  }

  .ticker__track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    animation: ticker-run 84s linear infinite;
  }

  .ticker__track span {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.3em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #fff;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  @keyframes ticker-run {
    from {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
  }

  /* Visually hidden but focusable — `display: none` would take it out of the
     tab order and there would be no keyboard route to the control. */
  .ticker__toggle {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Opaque and token-based, not a one-off rgba() — the full-width track scrolls
     continuously behind this button, and a translucent black over white
     category text measured 1.98:1 for the white label (3.35:1 on hover),
     both short of the WCAG 1.4.3 4.5:1 floor. --color-red-dark gives the label
     6.52:1 (handoff.md §3) regardless of what is passing underneath. */
  .ticker__btn {
    position: absolute;
    right: 8px;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #fff;
    background: var(--color-red-dark);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
  }

  .ticker__btn:hover {
    background: var(--color-red-deep);
  }

  /* The focus ring is driven off the input, because the label is what is
     visible and the input is what receives focus. Inset, not outset: the band
     is only 46.8px tall and clips with `overflow: hidden`, so an outward
     offset put both horizontal strokes of the ring outside the clip region.
     currentColor, not a literal #fff: the button's `color` flips between #fff
     and --color-red-deep with the checked state, and a literal white ring
     went invisible (1.00:1) against the checked state's white fill. Tying the
     ring to `color` covers both states with one declaration and cannot drift
     if the checked palette changes later. */
  .ticker__toggle:focus-visible + .ticker__btn {
    outline: 2px solid currentColor;
    outline-offset: -2px;
  }

  /* The word swap (Pause/Play) that used to live here was removed: the
     accessible name is now the static aria-label on the input, and swapping
     the button's visible word out of sync with that name is exactly the
     Label in Name failure this file's header warns about. The checked state
     is now a sighted-only cue. --color-red-deep on #fff (what this button
     actually sits on) is 9.07:1 — handoff.md §3's 8.40:1 is the same pair
     measured against --color-paper, a slightly darker surface, not this one.

     The pause mechanism below is `:has()` and nothing else. Without support
     the band still scrolls, the checkbox still toggles, and there is no way
     to stop it — a WCAG 2.2.2 failure plus a control that lies. Accepted
     because `:has()` has been Baseline since Firefox 121 (Dec 2023) and the
     repo declares no browser floor below that. Anything that drops these
     rules has to replace the mechanism, not just the styling. */
  .ticker:has(.ticker__toggle:checked) .ticker__track {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }
  .ticker:has(.ticker__toggle:checked) .ticker__btn {
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--color-red-deep);
  }

  /* WHERE HOVERING EXISTS, THE CONTROL IS REVEALED BY IT.
     Requested 2026-08-13: the button was visually noisy sitting permanently on
     the band. Hiding it costs nothing here because a pointer can bring it back,
     and `:focus-within` covers the keyboard route — WCAG 2.2.2 asks for a
     mechanism, not a permanently visible one.

     `opacity`, not `display: none`: the label must keep its box so the reveal
     does not reflow the band, and the checkbox beside it must stay focusable.
     `pointer-events` follows the opacity so an invisible button is never a
     click target. */
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .ticker__btn {
      opacity: 0;
      pointer-events: none;
      transition: opacity var(--dur-base) var(--ease-out);
    }

    .ticker:hover .ticker__btn,
    .ticker:focus-within .ticker__btn {
      opacity: 1;
      pointer-events: auto;
    }
  }

  /* TOUCH: NO CONTROL, AND THE BAND STILL SCROLLS.
     KNOWN, ACCEPTED WCAG 2.2.2 FAILURE — decided by the client 2026-08-13
     after the cost was put to them twice. Do not "fix" it silently; it is a
     decision, and reversing it is theirs to make.

     `hover` does not exist on a touch screen, so unlike the desktop rule above
     there is nothing that can bring a hidden control back — hidden here means
     absent. The band therefore moves, indefinitely, with no mechanism to pause
     it, which is a Level A failure for exactly the readers the mechanism
     existed for: anyone who cannot track or tune out moving text.

     Two ways back, if it is ever reversed: stop the animation here too
     (`animation: none` on `.ticker__track`, which removes the obligation along
     with the motion, as the reduced-motion block below does), or restore the
     control. Hiding it more cleverly is not a third option.

     `prefers-reduced-motion` still wins below, so a visitor who has asked their
     device for less motion gets a static band regardless of this. */
  @media (hover: none) {
    .ticker__toggle,
    .ticker__btn {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  /* The band does not move at all here, so there is nothing to pause and the
     control is removed rather than left as a lie.

     `src/styles/global.css` collapses every animation site-wide to 0.01ms and a
     single iteration with `!important`, which no scoped rule can outrank. An
     opt-in was tried and cannot work: `animation-play-state` cannot restart an
     animation that has already run to completion, so the button flipped its
     label and the band stayed still. WCAG 2.2.2 is not engaged when nothing
     moves, and a pause control for static content is noise. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ticker__toggle,
    .ticker__btn {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
